 episode over  the fighter jets from poland to ukraine  illustrates the importance of  consultation and that behind the scenes  uh private discussion and coordination  and what's probably surprising is that  you haven't had more um sort of  incidents like this and in fact it's  been across the board unified response  in a very complicated situation  with all of that uh  equipment that you're talking about  there that's gone from europe and from  the u.s to  uh  ukraine how matched or otherwise are  russia's and ukraine's military on the  ground and also in the air  well  you know the assumption before february  24th was that  it would take anywhere between 24 to 72  hours for  ukrainian defense forces to fold against  uh russia's technologically advanced  military but in fact the ukrainian army  has  proven uh the that has the will to fight  it has the resolve and the ability to do  so and not only the defense forces but  of course you've seen these territorial  defense forces civilians volunteers  uh and across the ukrainian population a  unified response and commitment to  resist  now  the weaponry that's being delivered to  ukraine is important because uh  president zielinski has declared that  they will fight they will not surrender  they will fight uh until russia backs  down but they need the tools to do so  and in fact uh the west and the free  world at large has become the arsenal of  ukraine's democracy and resistance just  as uh america during world war ii was  the arsenal of democracy and helping the  uk and other  uh european allies resist uh nazi  aggression  all right thank you very much for your  time bart chef chick there visiting  senior fellow at the german martial fund  thank you very much  russia and ukraine have collectively  long been referred to as the breadbasket  of europe together they account for 30  of global wheat exports but the war in  ukraine is pushing up the price of grain  around the world especially in heavily  dependent regions like africa and the  middle east the united nations food and  agricultural organization says egypt  imports around 85 percent of its wheat  from the two warring sides  lebanon gets 81 percent of its wheat  from ukraine  other countries like iran libya and  turkey are also heavily reliant  food shortages could affect the lives of  millions of people particularly in  developing countries steep price  increases and basic necessities like  bread milk and meat can also produce  food insecurity famine and civil unrest  let's bring in abdullah abbasian he is a  food market expert and former senior  economist at fao and former g20  agricultural market information system  secretary that's a mouthful uh he joins  us via zoom from rome thank you for your  time um what's your assessment of what  this is going to mean  for those countries whose populations  already face food insecurity  unfortunately it's going to be more  difficult  you know the prices have been going up  for basically all basic foodstuffs uh at  least for a year  well before the current war uh the war  is just exacerbating the the pressure on  prices to go up further because you know  people naturally expect that the uh  there will be a shortfall  inevitabilities in the world market and  this will be seen in prices wheat for  example which is so important for middle  eastern north african markets have  gained something like 40 just recently i  mean this type of increase in such a  speed has taken everybody by surprise  and i'm afraid that many countries  around the world  are not prepared for that  and i was reading that some countries uh  traditionally have consumed a lot of  wheat because wheat  has been cheaper than protein so what do  you think this is going to mean for  people's health i mean longer term  well again going back to the kobe period  which still is with us by the way people  seem to forget that  we already have seen a huge increase in  the number of hungry and as well as huge  increase in malnourishment that means  you're not getting the kind of equality  food that you need this predates the war  now with these prices i'm just afraid  that not even bread may become  affordable in many places in the world  just  minutes ago in the us they came up with  the latest inflation  level it's a striking 7.9 percent year  on year fastest increase in 40 years and  we're talking here about highly  developed country united states so you  could just imagine  the pressure right now in many parts of  the world when it comes to inflation and  inflation is not just food it's energy  it's everything so this is also the  other thing that we have to really  really contemplate on we're not talking  about food in isolation  look at the fertilizer market look at  the energy market all these things come  together so the pressure on especially  the most vulnerable  i do not think has been greater than  this since the second world war  are there alternative sources i know  you're saying that it's not just about  wheat it has flow-on effects everywhere  but i guess my question is  is this shorter is this price increase  uh  caused by a shortage able to be easily  circumvented by planting more elsewhere  or like how long does it take to to make  up for that loss  i'm glad you asked this question because  it's very important if you look at the  short term prayers let's say between now  and the next big harvests in the  northern hemisphere talking about grains  as a matter of fact we shouldn't be so  worried about the shortage ukraine and  russia have already exported the bulk of  what they would have exported in the  current marketing season known as 20 20  21.  um there is probably like eight nine ten  million ton left to to be exported  before the next harvest and i don't  think that the rest of the world other  major exporters would not be able to  make up for that unfortunately though  the market doesn't look at this it  doesn't look at what is happening the  next two three months he's looking  beyond he's worried about planting the  spring plantings for next year is  worried about whether the fact in these  countries especially in ukraine i would  say there will be ordinary harvesting  that will take place and that next year  ukraine will be able to export or not or  how much of russian wheat will make it  to the world market this anticipation of  next year next season i would rather say  which is 20 20 23 season next season not  having as much as even this year is what  is fueling the price increase um it's  inevitable uh we're all humans we're all  worried about the future what do we do  we go and buy now we try to buy now and  there is a there is a bit of a  speculation there  which is also fueling so i'm very  worried about this because the prospects  do not look good but if you want to be  realistic we have to accept the fact  that today as we speak we do not have a  shortage what we have is lots of fear  and that fear has added is is the one  that drives on prices  all right hey thank you very much uh for  your time there abdul there's a abbasian  food market expert  thank you

